[al:新概念英语（三）]
[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:Funny or Not?]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.30]Lesson 29 | 第29课
[00:02.23]Funny or not? | 是否好笑
[00:09.34]What is the basis of 'sick' humour? | “病态幽默”的基础是什么？
[00:14.46]Whether we find a joke funny or not largely depends on where we have been brought up. | 我们觉得一个笑话是否好笑，很大程度上取决于我们是在哪儿长大的。
[00:21.14]The sense of humour is mysteriously bound up with national characteristics. | 幽默感与民族特性神秘地联系在一起。
[00:27.58]A Frenchman, for instance, might find it hard to laugh at a Russian joke. | 譬如，法国人很难听懂一个俄国笑话。
[00:33.15]In the same way, a Russian might fail to see anything amusing in a joke | 同样，一个俄国人也许看不出一个
[00:38.74]which would make an Englishman laugh to tears. | 能令英国人笑出眼泪的笑话有什么可笑之处。
[00:43.08]Most funny stories are based on comic situations. | 大多数滑稽故事都依赖于喜剧性的情景。
[00:47.99]In spite of national differences, certain funny situations have a universal appeal. | 尽管民族不同，有些滑稽的情景却具有普遍的感染力。
[00:54.56]No matter where you live, | 无论你生活在哪里，
[00:56.47]you would find it difficult not to laugh at, say, Charlie Chaplin's early films. | 你都会发现很难不对查理·卓别林早期的电影发笑。
[01:03.16]However, a new type of humour, which stems largely from U.S., has recently come into fashion. | 然而，近来一种主要源自美国的新型幽默流行了起来。
[01:11.51]It is called 'sick humour'. Comedians base their jokes on tragic situations like violent death or serious accidents. | 它被称为“病态幽默”。喜剧演员根据悲剧性的情景——如暴死或重大事故——来编造笑话。
[01:22.68]Many people find this sort of joke distasteful. | 许多人觉得这种笑话令人厌恶。
[01:27.30]The following example of 'sick humour' will enable you to judge for yourself. | 下面的“病态幽默”的例子能让你自己作出判断。
[01:33.87]A man who had broken his right leg was taken to hospital a few weeks before Christmas. | 圣诞节前几周，一个人摔断了右腿被送进医院。
[01:40.55]From the moment he arrived there, | 从入院之时起，
[01:42.86]he kept on pestering his doctor to tell him when he would be able to go home. | 他就缠着医生，让医生告诉他什么时候可以回家。
[01:48.84]He dreaded having to spend Christmas in hospital. | 他害怕不得不在医院过圣诞节。
[01:52.72]Though the doctor did his best, the patient's recovery was slow. | 尽管医生竭尽全力，病人的恢复仍很缓慢。
[01:57.98]On Christmas day, the man still had his right leg in plaster. | 圣诞节那天，他的右腿还打着石膏。
[02:03.68]He spent a miserable day in bed thinking of all the fun he was missing. | 他在床上痛苦地躺了一天，惦念着错过的一切乐趣。
[02:09.44]The following day, however, the doctor consoled him by telling him | 然而第二天，医生安慰他说，
[02:14.27]that his chances of being able to leave hospital in time for New Year celebrations were good. | 他有望赶上新年庆祝活动及时出院。
[02:21.56]The man took heart and sure enough, on New Year's Eve he was able to hobble along to a party. | 那人来了精神。果然，除夕那天他得以拖着腿去参加了一个晚会。
[02:29.68]To compensate for his unpleasant experiences in hospital, | 为了补偿住院期间的不愉快经历，
[02:34.63]the man drank a little more than was good for him. | 那人多喝了几杯，超出了对他有益的量。
[02:38.28]In the process, he enjoyed himself thoroughly and kept telling everybody how much he hated hospitals. | 在这个过程中，他玩得尽兴，不停地告诉每个人他是多么讨厌医院。
[02:47.15]He was still mumbling something about hospitals at the end of the party when he slipped on a piece of ice and broke his left leg. | 晚会结束时，他还在嘟囔着有关医院的事，这时他滑倒在一片冰上，摔断了左腿。
